MobileGlues: BC4 and BC5 kept as EAC R11 and RG11, at their own size; empty levels filled without the encoder.

Where OpenGL ES has no RGTC (Mali, PowerVR), the game's BC4 and BC5 textures were decoded to R8 and RG8: twice the
memory of the blocks. A Xiaomi 12T (Mali-G610, 7.4 GB) loading High sprites made 8 BC4 atlases as 368 MB of R8 and 4
BC5 ones as 155 MB of RG8, ran out of memory and the kernel restarted the phone. OpenGL ES 3.0 has EAC R11 and RG11 on
every GPU, at BC4's and BC5's 4 and 8 bits a texel: each block is decoded and encoded again on the GPU, in the ASTC
encoder's batches, by an EAC encoder written from Khronos' format spec. Tried on BC4 blocks against a decoder from the
spec: 0.8 off (of 255) on gradients, 0.5 on shadow edges' full 0-255 ramps. The encoder's batches now count in words,
as EAC R11 blocks are 8 bytes. When the encoder is built, a 64x8 level of each is encoded and drawn back; drawn wrong,
BC4 and BC5 are decoded as before, and MG_BC_EAC=0 does that too, to compare.

An empty level (Factorio makes every atlas empty, then fills it) was queued for the encoder to fill with transparent
blocks, and waited in its output buffer with every other level made in a row, a second copy of them: with BC4 and BC5
queued too, the 12T's Medium load went 700 MB higher for two seconds as the atlases were made. Now it's filled from
memory with a constant block, 1 MB of rows at a time, and that peak is gone.

